  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently there are lingering showers areas south of Tucson. \nSimilar to previous nights, there is not much in the way of \nprecipitation with accumulations less than 0.10 inches and a \nrumble of thunder or two. More lingering showers are to the \nnorth of the area in the White Mountains that will be similar \nand slowly move southwest. Depending on how long this cloud \ncover will stick around will help to see how much heating and \ninstability we can tap into this afternoon and evening. \n\nLooking closer at this afternoon and evening, the high pressure\nremains in a favorable position with NE to SW flow with modest\nflow at 15-20 kts of storm motion. Compared to the set up from \nyesterday, the best chances for storm activity have slightly \nmoved westward, with Tucson on the western edge. With the 00Z \nCAMs, so far the HRRR and FV3 are the only models bringing in \nthe storms that develop off the White Mountains, so we can't \nrule out the possibility, but as of now the better shot at \nstorms in Tucson is on Monday. The storms for today, continue to\nbe more of a severe threat of strong outflow winds and areas of\nblowing dust than a flash flooding threat as storms will be \nmoving. The 00Z HREF is still continuing to highlight a 10 \npercent probability of 50 kts in this area.\n\nMonday is beginning to shape up to be a more active day for much\nof Southeastern Arizona. The high pressure will begin to\nelongate to the west and re-center over Southern\nCalifornia/Southeastern Arizona, creating an area of deformation\nand lift over Southeastern Arizona. The 00Z HREF is showing a\ngreater area of 10 percent contour of 50 kts. Blowing dust can\nbe more of an issue Monday afternoon and evening as well. SPC\nhas begun to highlight the area with a marginal risk for severe\nwinds.\n\nNext week remains to look like an elevated pattern, with \neasterly flow allowing for continuous moisture and enough motion\nfor the atmosphere to reset each day. With the continuous \nmoisture flow, with an eventual shift from a wind threat to \nmore of a flash flood threat, but still too soon to pinpoint \nwhen that will be.\n\n\nOutside of storms, the heat remains in place through this\nweekend into early next week with temperatures 6 to 12 degrees \nabove normal. Continue to practice heat safety over the coming \ndays. The area begins to 'cool' to 3 to 6 degrees above normal \nTuesday onward.",
